+In polar coordinates, the 2D Fourier transform is:
+$$ F^{2D}(\psi, \nu) = \int_{0}^{2\pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(r \cos \phi, r \sin \phi) |r| e^{-2i\pi\nu r \cos(\phi-\psi)} \, dr \, d\phi $$
+where $X = \nu \cos \psi$ and $Y = \nu \sin \psi$ [S2, unofficial]. Equating the 1D and 2D forms yields the theorem:
+$$ F_\theta(\nu) = F^{2D}(f(r \cos \phi, r \sin \phi))|_{\psi=\theta} $$
+which confirms that the 1D Fourier transform of the projection equals the radial line at angle $\theta$ in the 2D Fourier space [S2, unofficial].
+
 **Parameters**
 - $\nu$: Spatial frequency variable. Maps to coordinates $(\nu \cos \theta, \nu \sin \theta)$ in the 2D frequency plane [S1, unofficial].
